    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\nA deep copy duplicates nested structures instead of reusing their references.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nIdentify the rule, trace the smallest example, and verify the edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nfunction deepClone<T>(value: T): T {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\") return value;\n  if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(deepClone) as T;\n\n  const result: Record<string, unknown> = {};\n  for (const key in value as object) {\n    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(value, key)) {\n      result[key] = deepClone((value as Record<string, unknown>)[key]);\n    }\n  }\n  return result as T;\n}\n\nconst copy = deepClone({\n  profile: { name: \"Rasik\" },\n});\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / PRACTICAL TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst copy = structuredClone({\n  profile: { name: \"Rasik\" },\n});\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\nindependent nested object\n```\n\nWhy: The recursive implementation copies nested objects/arrays.\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck empty/nullish values, mutation vs copying, invalid inputs, ordering, browser/Node differences, and cleanup/memory retention where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the runtime rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and production trade-off.",
